      "id": "session:2026-10-20-84137a84",
      "type": "Session",
      "label": "Unofficial LLM council on the scrapy loss, step 0 of the re-evaluation (#67, PR #68), lean control, and project concluded with a published report (#69, PR #70)",
      "description": "Goal: decide whether to revive kg after the graph lost on scrapy; then run the cheapest decisive test and close or continue.\nInstructions: council analysis was unofficial (nothing to the repo) until the user said 'write it as an issue and start'; no API keys, subagents only; user merges PRs himself; user asked to close v0.3 issues and the v0.3 and v0.1 milestones, and to record that the project is concluded.\nDiscoveries: load_graph re-validated the 11.5 MB scrapy store on every read (5-10 s) and matplotlib was always imported; agents tried --depth 0 19 times; with kg fast, companion (code tools + kg) scores 0.96 vs code 0.98, -40% context, +45% time, so the step-0 gate fails on time; the lean control shows the context saving outside kg-using runs comes from a prompt line; where kg is used it halves context at equal score but costs a third more time; kg never improves accuracy.\nAccomplished: issue #67 and PR #68 (validation cache, lazy render import, --depth 0, companion and lean strategies, 96 subagent runs) merged; issue #69 and PR #70 (docs/report.md, bench README 'Measure your own tool', README/roadmap/site say concluded) merged; #35, #52, #55, #60, #66 closed; milestones v0.1 and v0.3 closed; no open issues remain.\nNext steps: none planned; the project is concluded. If reopened, start from the open idea of dotted-path string reference edges, preferably contributed to an existing tool.\nRelevant files: docs/report.md, docs/benchmark.md, docs/roadmap.md, bench/README.md, src/kg/store.py, src/kg/render/__init__.py, bench/tools.py",
