    {
      "id": "experiment:llm-council-on-scrapy-loss",
      "type": "Experiment",
      "label": "LLM council (Karpathy protocol via subagents): 5 of 5 experts say PIVOT; cheapest decisive step is fix speed and rerun the same questions",
      "description": "What: karpathy/llm-council's 3-stage protocol (independent opinions, anonymous cross-review and ranking, chairman) run with mixed-model subagents instead of OpenRouter; 5 experts (evidence auditor, landscape, KG beyond code, devil's advocate, graph engineer), 5 reviewers, chairman.\nWhy: the user wanted an unofficial analysis of whether to revive kg after scrapy, and what similar tools exist; llm-council is a FastAPI app needing an OpenRouter key, so only its protocol was reused.\nWhere: session scratchpad council/ (FINAL.md, stage1/, stage2/, aggregate_rankings.md), not in the repo; the plan became issue #67.\nLearned: verdict bounded PIVOT (65%): test kg as a complement to grep, not a replacement; reviewers ranked engineer and auditor first, KG-beyond-code last; split between enriching the code layer (A, E) and keeping only a docs/decisions drift layer (B, C, D); code-graph-for-agents space is crowded (codegraph, GitNexus, codebase-memory-mcp, Serena); Codebase-Memory paper: graph 83% vs file exploration 92%; RepoGraph's +32.8% is relative (~2 points absolute); TYPE_CHECKING import flags already exist in grimp; possibly novel: dotted-path string edges (~50%) and documented-vs-code defaults check (~40%); main risk: all participants share the model family with the benchmark agent and question author, and demand rests on one client.",
      "attrs": {
        "status": "done",
        "session": "session:2026-10-20-84137a84",
        "created": "2026-10-20"
      },
